# Copy to `.env.local` and fill in. `.env.local` is gitignored and stays that way.
#
# NOTHING IN HERE IS A VALUE. This file records which variables exist and what
# each is for; the values live on your machine and in Vercel's environment
# settings, and never in a chat transcript or a commit.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database — feature 005 T002
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Neon, POOLED connection string — the host contains "-pooler". Neon is the
# database for EVERY environment, tests included. The direct string works fine
# locally and exhausts the connection limit under serverless concurrency, which
# is the worst failure schedule: it passes every test and breaks under traffic.
DATABASE_URL=

# Neon, DIRECT connection string. MIGRATIONS ONLY, and the opposite of the rule
# above on purpose. Neon's pooler is PgBouncer in transaction mode, where a
# connection returns to the pool at the end of each transaction — so anything
# expecting to outlive one, like a migration runner's advisory lock, silently
# stops meaning what it says. Neon hands out both strings for exactly this.
#
# Neon pastes this into your env block automatically as DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED.
# It also pastes ~13 PG*/POSTGRES_* aliases that this project never reads —
# delete them. A variable nothing reads is a variable nobody maintains.
DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Identity — feature 005 T012 onward
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Google OAuth **web application** client. Only the ID is needed: we verify
# Google's ID tokens against their published JWKS rather than running an OAuth
# code exchange, so there is no client secret on the server and no credential of
# any kind in the client build.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=

# The signing key for OUR OWN tokens — not Google's. Generate a fresh one:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
# Rotating this invalidates every live session, which is the intended blast
# radius for a suspected leak.
JWT_SIGNING_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The origin boundary — two projects, two variables
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `apps/api` and `apps/client` are separate Vercel projects, so the browser is
# always making a cross-origin request. These two variables are the only thing
# joining them, and each is set on ONE project.
#
# --- set on the API project ---
#
# Comma-separated, exact-match, no wildcards. `*` and `null` are DROPPED rather
# than honoured (`apps/api/src/cors.ts`) — they are the two values that turn an
# allowlist into an open door, and neither ever arrives on purpose.
#
# UNSET IN PRODUCTION MEANS NO BROWSER CAN CALL THE API. There is no default and
# that is deliberate: defaulting to localhost would make any developer with
# something on port 5173 a permitted caller of the live API. `pnpm dev` supplies
# the local origins from `src/dev.ts`, which never ships to Vercel — so nothing
# needs to be set here to work on a laptop.
#
#   CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://lmntlz.vercel.app
CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=

# --- set on the CLIENT project, at BUILD time ---
#
# Vite inlines anything prefixed `VITE_` into the bundle, so this is baked in
# when the client is built and cannot be changed by an env edit afterwards — a
# change here needs a redeploy, not a restart.
#
# EMPTY MEANS SAME-ORIGIN, which is right for a bundle that might be served from
# anywhere and wrong for the two-project deployment. Set it to the API's origin,
# with no trailing slash and no `/v1` — `apps/client/src/lib/api.ts` adds that.
#
#   VITE_API_BASE_URL=https://lmntlz.vercel.app
VITE_API_BASE_URL=

# --- also set on the CLIENT project, at BUILD time ---
#
# The SAME value as GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID above. It appears twice because the two
# halves are two Vercel projects and neither can read the other's environment.
#
# **This is an identifier, not a credential**, and baking it into a public
# bundle is how Google intends it to be used: the browser needs it to ask Google
# for an ID token, and the server verifies that token against Google's published
# JWKS. There is no client secret in this project at all —
# `apps/api/tests/auth/clientSecrecy.test.ts` scans the built bundle and asserts
# zero matches for one.
#
# Unset means the sign-in panel renders an explicit "this build has no client
# ID" message rather than a button that silently does nothing. Vite only inlines
# a VITE_ variable that appears in the source, so setting this changes nothing
# until the client is rebuilt.
VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Realtime transport — Ably — feature 014
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# One key, set on the API PROJECT ONLY. Ably's format is `name:secret`; the key
# itself identifies which Ably app it belongs to, so there is no separate
# sandbox/production switch to get wrong — a sandbox key reaches the sandbox app
# and nothing else.
#
# ***NEVER GIVE THIS A `VITE_` PREFIX, AND NEVER SET IT ON THE CLIENT.***
#
# This is the one variable in the project where leaking it is not merely a
# credential leak, it is a hole in the game's economy. Vite inlines anything
# prefixed `VITE_` straight into a public bundle. An Ably API key in a browser is
# a PUBLISH-CAPABLE credential, and a client that can publish to a channel puts a
# message in front of every subscriber WITHOUT passing through the route that
# charges shards for it. There is no reconciliation that gets those back.
#
# The whole chat design exists to make that impossible: the browser never holds
# an Ably key at all. It calls `GET /v1/chat/token`, and our API mints a
# SUBSCRIBE-ONLY token scoped to exactly the channels that player may read.
# `apps/api/src/chat/transport.ts` has no method that returns a publishing
# credential at any privilege level, and `tests/chat/publishCredential.test.ts`
# fails if one is ever added.
#
# UNSET IS A LEGITIMATE STATE and is the default for a laptop. With no key the
# in-memory broker is used — which is not a test double, it is what the entire
# feature is developed and tested against. Chat works, nothing errors, and only
# delivery to a real second browser is missing.
ABLY_API_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Paddle MCP — DEVELOPER TOOLING, not application runtime — feature 011
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ***THIS ONE IS DIFFERENT FROM EVERY VARIABLE ABOVE, AND `.env.local` WILL NOT
# WORK FOR IT.*** No application code reads it. It is consumed by `.mcp.json` at
# the repo root, which Claude Code expands from ITS OWN PROCESS ENVIRONMENT —
# this project's env files are never consulted. Putting it in `.env.local`
# produces a server that loads, shows no error, and 401s on the first call.
#
# Set it as a real user environment variable instead, then restart VS Code
# COMPLETELY (reloading the window does not re-read the environment):
#
#   setx PADDLE_SANDBOX_API_KEY "pdl_sdbx_..."
#
# It is listed here because this file's job is to record which variables exist,
# and the wrong home for it is the first thing anyone would try.
#
# SANDBOX ONLY, AND DELIBERATELY SO. Paddle publishes a second server at
# `mcp.paddle.com` for live, which is NOT configured in `.mcp.json` and should
# not be. That server gates nothing on its own — the API key's scopes are the
# only permission boundary — so a live key there makes destructive writes
# against real customers and real money reachable from an ordinary tool call.
# The sandbox catalog is entirely separate, so nothing is lost by staying in it.
# A `pdl_sdbx_` key is rejected by the live URL and vice versa, which means the
# prefix is a genuine safety check: if the key does not start `pdl_sdbx_`, it is
# the wrong key and does not belong in this variable.
#
# Both keys were minted 2026-07-30. Paddle expires a key 90 days after issue, so
# these lapse around 2026-10-28 — most likely before the store work starts, and a
# lapsed key presents as an ordinary auth failure rather than anything labelled
# "expired". Re-mint rather than debug if it stops working after that date.
PADDLE_SANDBOX_API_KEY=

# The live key. NOT referenced by `.mcp.json` and NOT promoted to the machine
# environment — it sits here, unused, purely so the value is not lost. That is
# deliberate, per the paragraph above: no tool call should be able to reach
# production. When live payments genuinely go in, this belongs in the API
# project's Vercel environment, read by server code that charges money on
# purpose — never in an MCP header, and never with a `VITE_` prefix.
PADDLE_LIVE_API_KEY=
